\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Let $\mathbb P^n$ be complex projective $n$-space, with $n>1$. The source calls a rational self-map $f$ critically finite if there are possibly reducible hypersurfaces $V\subset W\subset\mathbb P^n$ for which
\[f:\mathbb P^n\setminus W\longrightarrow\mathbb P^n\setminus V\]
is a covering map. A complex manifold is Kobayashi hyperbolic when its Kobayashi pseudodistance separates distinct points.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Are there nontrivial examples of such critically finite rational maps for which $\mathbb P^n\setminus V$ is Kobayashi hyperbolic? If so, describe their dynamics. \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9201271v1}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Find nontrivial critically finite projective maps with a Kobayashi-hyperbolic postcritical complement, and describe their dynamics.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 5300089 (AMR-052-0089). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Ben Bielefeld (editor) and conference contributors, \emph{Conformal Dynamics Problem List}, arXiv:math/9201271v1 (1990), Curt McMullen, \S5, Critically finite rational maps on $\mathbb P^n$, Problem, printed p. 12. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9201271v1}.
\end{description}
